e have a bunch of different  resources so for instance we have a  bunch of illustrated demos and  specifically that problem that i was  describing before  with science social networks you can you  can we have an illustrated demo around  this that i've  adapted for the purpose of this  presentation so you can for instance see  the science social network you can see  it being divided into two rivers romeo  and juliet  and you can start learning about  frustration and then finally um you know  you can see how uh in romeo and juliet  there is no way to create an  unfrustrated problem  and then finally you can learn about how  we uh program this problem directly onto  the quantum computer  i'll also mention that you know if you  have particular applications that you're  interested in understanding how you  might solve using a quantum computer we  have a set of examples and you know  there's lots of different examples of  different sorts of problem classes but  i'm going to continue on the science  social network theme  and give that a click uh here if you  open that up you can go check out how we  solve that problem on github or you can  open this up in our online integrated  developer environment which is uh an  online place that we've pre-installed  everything that you need in order to  access uh quantum computing so it has  ocean our open source tools it has your  access to the quantum computer  pre-configured it has all the libraries  that you need to to have installed and  really everything that you need it's a  full featured ide  um and then the last thing that i'll  just you know mention is that  one of the reasons that we've done this  is in order to make this  easier for users to understand how the  quantum computer is getting involved so  for instance i can i can solve that same  structural imbalance problem that i was  talking about before  and i'm going to solve it on the qpu  and  in this case let's say that i'm  interested in how did i map this  structural 