r in its particular  problem domain now quantum computers  have some different complexities than  classical computers so gpus and cpus  exist in the same complexity class  quantum computers have their own  complexity class so in some ways it's  different but there isn't really a  problem that can't be expressed  in terms of problems that can't be  solved um i would have to point you to  the google supremacy result from a few  years ago there's some controversy  around was that really a supremacy  resulted it'll take a little time for  that to shake out but it doesn't make it  it's still a very cool scientific result  of a example of a problem that  can't be solved classically it can be  expressed but not solved or at least not  within  reasonable timelines um  so yeah i hope that answers the question  i'm happy to follow up if there's if  there's more questions about that  can i ill oh sorry great question um so  can i illustrate how a frustration  problem manifests in real world  applications um  that's an awesome question so  let's imagine for a moment that i have  an unfrustrated problem so i'm gonna  solve a social science social networking  problem um where  everybody is friends  there's no there's no hostility at all  and i want to assign my agents to one of  two teams in such a way that friends  play together and enemies play across  different teams well that problem's  trivial right like i just i place  somebody on a team and then i put their  friend on their team and then they're  their friend on their team and i just  carry on and there's no  difficulty in that problem because  there's no trade-offs there's no um  there's no difficulty to it  whereas in a highly frustrated problem  frustration really just means trade-offs  like i'm having to balance between  these two  people uh liking each other being really  good friends and then but they both  don't like this other person and how do  you work that out so  real difficult problems in industry are  frustra