al side whereas the vendors who  are trying to actually build these  things and make it useful for business i  mean we're laser focused on how do we  make this useful now now now now and so  in general my advice would be to start  with the vendors if you're interested in  getting more more resources  hi alex can i jump in with a live  question  yeah of course great um thank you for  the talk um i was just curious if the  hardware that you showed us today  allows you to also think about quantum  error correction in addition to solving  problems via annealing  yeah that's a great question um  the short answer is  in the annealing model you don't really  need quantum error correction in the  same way that you do in a circuit model  or gate model system  so our system starts off in  superposition sort of in a coherence  regime and then it ends the computation  in the classical regime so it doesn't  need to stay coherent throughout the  entire computation  and so as that's an advantage to us  because uh it means that you need you  don't need error correction in the same  way now  that said  many of the same  principles and aspects of fabrication do  apply to quantum annealing it's actually  the the fabrication and the actual  implementation of the systems are much  more similar than i think a lot of  people realize um so  even though the particular model that  we've implemented  doesn't doesn't really use that concept  wholesale it does use a lot of the  underlying concepts and um  yeah maybe i'll just leave it at that  for now although i will mention that we  have our  conference starting uh next week where  we're announcing some really cool new  stuff um which uh unfortunately i can't  talk about because we're a whole like  three days earlier but i would really  encourage you to check that out when we  when we talk about it in about three or  four days  we have time for one more question  uh should i read one off slack or  yeah sure okay um how quickly did the  qpu sol